{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLMVZyNhsRsCnstvsWFM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018Xu6Hjd7T1ERadzswodTre","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/proposal.md'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace('''Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.''','''Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.''',1)\nold='At the cap the controller stops claiming and posts once on the status issue.'\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,'''The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. `--loops` accepts `budget` as a name, but the gate is part of the pool and is always on. Posting the cap on the status issue is not part of this change.''',1)\ns=s.replace('budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2','budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its o